        exp: "Rule: Under UCC § 2-210(2) and UCC § 9-406(d), a term in an agreement between an account debtor and an assignor that prohibits, restricts, or requires the consent of the account debtor to the assignment of an account or a right to payment for goods sold or services rendered is ineffective as a matter of law. Even where the contract uses explicit language of nullity or voidness ('any assignment without consent shall be void'), modern commercial law and public policy favor the free liquidity and financing of commercial receivables. Once the seller has fully performed by manufacturing and delivering conforming goods that have been accepted, an anti-assignment clause cannot restrict the seller's right to assign the fully earned account receivable. The assignment to the factoring company is valid and enforceable (Option C). Options A and B are incorrect because statutory UCC rules override contractual anti-assignment language regarding fully earned rights to payment. Option D is incorrect because the statutory invalidation of anti-assignment terms operates automatically without proof of bad faith."
