        fp: "Defendant was in a dispute with a former business partner who had recently opened an independent auto repair garage. Intending to destroy the partner's business, Defendant drove to the garage at 1:00 AM on a Sunday. Defendant carried a 5-gallon container of kerosene and a heavy iron crowbar. Defendant used the crowbar to pry open a locked wooden side door, entered the customer waiting room, and splashed kerosene across the wooden service counter, upholstered waiting chairs, and drywall. Defendant struck a match, dropped it onto the kerosene-soaked counter, and watched a wall of flame erupt. The fire burned vigorously for ten minutes, completely charring and consuming the wooden service counter and the structural wood framing of the interior partition wall. The fire department arrived and extinguished the blaze before the exterior concrete block walls of the garage were damaged. The repair garage was a standalone commercial business structure that was never used for sleeping or human habitation. The jurisdiction defines arson according to its traditional common law elements.",
